  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 240 AM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThe flow aloft will remain WNW today as southerly level flow is \nacross the plains.  MLCAPE will rise up to 1000 j/kg over portions \nof the far ern plains by aftn.  Chances for tstms still look on the \nlow side as I can't find a focus for activity.  However, can't rule \nout an isold storm or two mainly across Lincoln county where a weak \nconvergence zone will exist this aftn.  Meanwhile, temps will begin \nto warm as readings reach the upper 80's to lower 90's over nern CO.\n\nOn Fri, zonal flow aloft will be over the area as a sfc lee trough \ndevelops over the plains.  This will lead to another warm day as \nhighs rise into the upper 80's to mid 90's over the plains. \nMeanwhile, models have some mid level moisture embedded in the flow, \nwhich may lead to isold high based showers/tstms over the higher \nterrain in the aftn.  Across the plains, MLCAPE is under 500 j/kg so \nany threat of showers/tstms will be on the low side.\n\nFor Fri night into Sat, zonal flow aloft will continue.  Meanwhile, \na cold front will move across Fri night into Sat morning.  This \nfront will drop temps back to more seasonal levels across nern CO. \nMLCAPE is fcst to be in the 500-800 j/kg across the plains with \nlower values across the higher terrain.  For now, have kept in a \nslight chc for showers/tstms across the higher terrain and along the \nI-25 Corridor for the aftn/evening hours.  Highs over nern CO will \nbe in the upper 70's to mid 80's.\n\nBy Sun, an upper level trough will move into the nrn Rockies with \nincreasing WSW flow aloft across the area.  At the sfc, a lee trough \nwill intensify east of the mtns with SSE low level flow across the \nplains.  MLCAPE is fcst to increase to 1000-1500 j/kg over portions \nof the plains east of a pseudo dryline. Thus may see a few \nstrong/svr storms by late aftn thru the evening hours. Highs on \nSun, will likely rise back to above normal levels.\n\nLooking ahead to Mon, WSW flow aloft will remain over the area. \nModels have backed off on the timing of another cold front moving \nacross as they now show it moving in Mon night into Tue morning.  If \nthis front does hold off until Mon night, then another day of above \nnormal temps would occur over the plains.  Meanwhile, may have a \nbetter chc of showers/tstms across the plains Mon night into Tue \nmorning as position of upper level jet combines with upslope flow \nbehind the front.  \n\nFor Tue, differences exist between the ECMWF and GFS with respect to \ntiming of additional precip and temps.  The ECMWF has a cool and \nstable airmass, over the plains, with highs only in the mid 60's to \nlower 70s.  Meanwhile, the GFS isn't as cool and has far more \nprecip.",
